<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>dansplugins</groupId>
    <artifactId>Fiefs</artifactId>
    <!-- Deliberately unchanged. Renaming the artifact would move the plugin's data directory
         (plugins/Fiefs/) and break anything declaring depend: [Fiefs]; the Medieval-Factions fork
         set the same precedent by keeping the com.dansplugins groupId. -->
    <version>0.11.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>Fiefs</name>
    <description>Sub-factions (fiefs) within Medieval Factions.</description>

    <properties>
        <!-- Build target. api-version in plugin.yml is a FLOOR, not a pin, so one jar built here
             loads on 26.1.x and 26.2 alike. -->
        <minecraft.version>26.1.2</minecraft.version>

        <!-- Pinned to the Paper-Version in mockbukkit-v26.1.2's jar manifest rather than the newest
             build, so that if a MockBukkit test tier is ever added, the API the tests mock is the API
             the code compiled against. Bump both together. -->
        <paper.version>26.1.2.build.72-stable</paper.version>

        <!-- Java 25 is the hard server minimum for Minecraft 26.1+, and paper-api ships class-file
             major 69, so 25 is also the floor for compiling against it. -->
        <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>

        <!-- Our Medieval-Factions fork, which carries the stable in-JVM API this plugin binds to
             (com.dansplugins.factionsystem.api.*). It is NOT on any public repository, so it must be
             installed into the local Maven repository first:

                 cd ../Medieval-Factions
                 JAVA_HOME=<jdk-17> ./gradlew publishToMavenLocal

             Build MF with JDK 17 (its Gradle 7.6.4 cannot run on JDK 25); the resulting Java 17
             bytecode runs fine on the Java 25 server. -->
        <medievalfactions.version>5.9.0-patriam.1-SNAPSHOT</medievalfactions.version>

        <!-- PatriamHeraldry's api module: the arms codec and the SubjectResolver contract. Like the
             Medieval-Factions api it is NOT on any public repository, so it must be installed into the
             local Maven repository first. Either from a clone:

                 cd ../PatriamHeraldry && ./mvnw -pl patriamheraldry-api -am install

             or from the copy committed to libs/, which is what CI uses because the repository is
             private and a workflow token cannot reach it:

                 cd libs && mvn install:install-file \
                     -Dfile=patriamheraldry-api-1.0.0.jar -DpomFile=patriamheraldry-api-1.0.0.pom

             See libs/README.md. Unlike MedievalFactions this is a SOFT dependency: see plugin.yml and
             dansplugins.fiefs.heraldry.HeraldryPresence. -->
        <patriamheraldry.version>1.0.0</patriamheraldry.version>

        <!-- MockBukkit 4.114.0 requires JUnit 6, not 5. -->
        <junit.version>6.1.0</junit.version>
        <mockbukkit.version>4.114.0</mockbukkit.version>

        <!-- The API artifact is unrelocated Kotlin, so tests that construct API types (FactionId) or
             implement MedievalFactionsApi need a plain kotlin-stdlib at runtime. Matches the Kotlin
             version the fork compiles with. -->
        <kotlin.version>1.7.0</kotlin.version>
    </properties>

    <repositories>
        <repository>
            <id>papermc</id>
            <url>https://repo.papermc.io/repository/maven-public/</url>
        </repository>

        <!-- Kept so a future PUBLISHED Medieval-Factions release resolves without editing the pom.
             The fork we currently build against comes from the local repository. -->
        <repository>
            <id>dansplugins</id>
            <url>https://repo.dansplugins.com/repository/maven-public/</url>
        </repository>
    </repositories>

    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.junit</groupId>
                <artifactId>junit-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${junit.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- paper-api rather than spigot-api: Patriam runs Paper, so this matches deployment, and it
             is what MockBukkit builds against if a test tier is added later. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.papermc.paper</groupId>
            <artifactId>paper-api</artifactId>
            <version>${paper.version}</version>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- The stable API only (24 KB), NOT the full 12.8 MB shaded plugin jar. Provided scope: the
             real plugin supplies these classes at runtime.

             This plugin binds ONLY to the stable api package, never to MF's internal services or
             models. That is not stylistic. Fiefs was written against MF4's internal API, MF5's
             Kotlin rewrite deleted it, and Fiefs stayed broken from 2022 until 2026.

             Using the api artifact rather than the shaded jar is also what makes tests possible: the
             shaded jar registers MF's own SLF4J provider through META-INF/services, which
             ServiceLoader initialises on the test classpath with MF not running, killing MockBukkit's
             ServerMock. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.dansplugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>medieval-factions-api</artifactId>
            <version>${medievalfactions.version}</version>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- PatriamHeraldry's api, so a fief can be a subject that bears arms. Provided, for the same
             reason the Medieval-Factions api is: PatriamHeraldry unpacks these classes into its own
             plugin jar and supplies them at runtime, so shading or bundling them here would put a
             second copy of SubjectResolver on the server and the ServicesManager lookup would then
             match nothing.

             It is optional as well as provided. Provided alone would be enough for the build, but
             `optional` is the honest declaration and it stops the api leaking onto the compile path of
             anything that depends on Fiefs. Nothing outside dansplugins.fiefs.heraldry may name these
             types; that package is guarded and the rest of the plugin must keep loading without it. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.exophobias</groupId>
            <artifactId>patriamheraldry-api</artifactId>
            <version>${patriamheraldry.version}</version>
            <scope>provided</scope>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Tests -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.platform</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-platform-launcher</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.mockbukkit.mockbukkit</groupId>
            <artifactId>mockbukkit-v26.1.2</artifactId>
            <version>${mockbukkit.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!-- `provided` is not on the test runtime classpath by default for transitive needs; the API
             jar's Kotlin classes need the stdlib when tests actually execute them. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jetbrains.kotlin</groupId>
            <artifactId>kotlin-stdlib</artifactId>
            <version>${kotlin.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <!-- Gson is deliberately NOT declared. Paper ships it on the server classpath and paper-api
         brings it in transitively at provided scope, so declaring or shading it would only risk
         loading a second copy at runtime. -->

    <build>
        <finalName>${project.name}</finalName>

        <resources>
            <!-- Filter ONLY plugin.yml. Filtering the whole resources directory means any stray ${...}
                 in a future data file is silently substituted at package time. -->
            <resource>
                <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
                <filtering>true</filtering>
                <includes>
                    <include>plugin.yml</include>
                </includes>
            </resource>
            <resource>
                <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
                <filtering>false</filtering>
                <excludes>
                    <exclude>plugin.yml</exclude>
                </excludes>
            </resource>
        </resources>

        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.13.0</version>
                <configuration>
                    <release>${maven.compiler.release}</release>
                    <compilerArgs>
                        <arg>-Xlint:all,-processing</arg>
                    </compilerArgs>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.5.2</version>
                <configuration>
                    <argLine>-Xshare:off</argLine>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>

        <!-- maven-shade-plugin is gone along with the Ponder dependency it existed to bundle. Every
             remaining dependency is `provided`, so the jar contains this plugin's classes and nothing
             else. -->
    </build>

    <profiles>
        <!--
          26.2 IS THE DEPLOYMENT TARGET. Patriam runs a stable 26.2, so this profile builds the jar
          that ships:

              .\mvnw.cmd "-Ppaper-26.2" clean package

          api-version in plugin.yml is a FLOOR rather than a pin, so one jar is ELIGIBLE to load on
          both 26.1.x and 26.2 - but eligibility is not compatibility. A method 26.2 removed is a
          NoSuchMethodError at the first call site that reaches it, and plugin.yml cannot express
          that. Compiling against the version actually deployed turns that class of failure into a
          build error.

          WHY THE DEFAULT IS STILL 26.1.2, WHICH LOOKS BACKWARDS AND IS NOT.
          MockBukkit publishes mockbukkit-v26.1.2 and, as of 2026-08-02, no 26.2 artifact exists.
          That is a limit of the TEST HARNESS, not a statement about what this plugin targets: a
          26.1.2 server mock cannot stand up against a 26.2 api classpath, and the suite run that
          way collapses into "Could not initialize class org.bukkit.Registry" errors that are the
          harness failing rather than the plugin. Making 26.2 the default would trade a meaningful
          green for a meaningless one.

          So the default keeps `mvnw clean test` honest and this profile keeps the shipped jar
          honest:

              .\mvnw.cmd clean test                       correctness gate, 26.1.2, runs the suite
              .\mvnw.cmd "-Ppaper-26.2" clean package     compatibility gate and the release jar

          Test EXECUTION is skipped here for the reason above. Test SOURCES still compile, which is
          where their value lies under this profile: after main, they are the largest body of api
          calls in the repository.

          Revisit the moment mockbukkit-v26.2 exists. At that point this profile becomes the
          default, the suite runs under it, and 26.1.2 becomes the compile-only nice-to-have.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>paper-26.2</id>
            <properties>
                <paper.version>26.2.build.92-stable</paper.version>
                <skipTests>true</skipTests>
            </properties>
        </profile>
    </profiles>
</project>
